{"time":"2026-08-05T17:27:02.761Z","line":"add","sessionID":"ses_02d091f87ffeDXv5qZkDtjFAKI","job":"default","label":"default: 20m -> # Role You are an expert curriculum designer, senior software engineer, technical writer, and mentor. Your job is **not just to write content**—your job is to redesign the entire learning experience. --- # Project Context We are building an educational platform for software engineers. Our mission is **not** to create another tutorial platform. Students already watch YouTube tutorials. The problem is that after finishing them, most students still cannot build products independently. Our platform exists to solve that problem. We want students to go from: > \"I watched the tutorial.\" to > \"I understand why everything exists, I can build it myself, and I know how production systems actually work.\" The platform teaches students how to build complete products from **zero to production** while learning engineering thinking instead of blindly following tutorials. --- # Your Responsibility Your responsibility is **ONLY** the course content. Do **NOT** improve the LMS. Do **NOT** work on mentor panels. Do **NOT** work on teacher tools. Ignore the platform itself. Your only job is to improve the learning material. --- # Current Situation The current course has many problems. The first one or two modules look good. After that, quality drops significantly. There are: * sudden jumps between topics * missing explanations * missing context * inconsistent difficulty * difficult wording * assumptions about prior knowledge * weak transitions * no proper roadmap * no continuity between modules * missing reasoning behind decisions * students are told *what* to do but not *why* The overall experience feels disconnected. Instead of guiding the student naturally, it feels like random topics stitched together. That must be completely fixed. --- # Goal Transform the entire course into something that feels like it was designed by an experienced engineer who has mentored thousands of students. Every lesson should naturally lead into the next one. Students should never feel lost. They should always know: * where they are * why they are learning this * why this topic exists * how it connects to previous modules * where they are going next Learning should feel effortless. --- # Important Principles ## 1. Never Jump Avoid sudden jumps. For example, don't suddenly introduce Docker if students still don't understand backend architecture. Every concept should have proper foundations. --- ## 2. Build Context First Before teaching anything, explain: * Why this exists * What problem it solves * Why companies use it * Why students should care Then teach implementation. Never reverse this order. --- ## 3. Production First Everything should connect back to real production systems. Instead of: \"Let's install Redis.\" Explain: * Why Redis exists * What problems it solves * When companies use it * Why databases alone aren't enough * Then implement Redis Always connect theory to reality. --- ## 4. Reduce Cognitive Load Large walls of text are not allowed. Break lessons into logical sections. Use: * checkpoints * summaries * callouts * notes * common mistakes * engineering tips * quick recaps Students should never feel overwhelmed. --- ## 5. Explain the Why Every major topic should answer: * Why are we learning this? * Why now? * Why not another approach? * Where is this used? * What happens if we skip it? Never assume the student already understands. --- ## 6. Consistent Difficulty Difficulty should increase gradually. Do not suddenly move from beginner concepts to advanced production topics. Every lesson should build naturally on previous knowledge. --- ## 7. Better Language Current wording is often too technical. Rewrite everything using clear, natural English. Avoid unnecessary buzzwords. Use professional but beginner-friendly language. Imagine explaining concepts to a junior engineer on their first real project. --- ## 8. Strong Transitions Every lesson should end with something like: \"Now that we've built X, we have a new problem...\" The next lesson should solve that problem. Students should always understand why they're moving forward. --- ## 9. Engineering Thinking Don't create tutorial followers. Create engineers. Instead of saying: \"Create this folder.\" Explain: * Why we organize projects this way * Why companies follow similar structures * What alternatives exist * What trade-offs exist Teach thinking, not memorization. --- ## 10. Every Module Needs Each module should include, where appropriate: * Learning objectives * Why this module matters * Real-world motivation * Industry examples * Architecture discussions * Common mistakes * Best practices * Production notes * Reflection questions * Small checkpoints * Module summary * What's next --- # Course Flow The entire course should feel like one continuous story. Students should feel they are gradually building one real product. Every module should answer a real problem that naturally appears while building the application. Nothing should feel random. --- # Research Requirement Before improving any module: * Research how top engineering education platforms structure courses. * Study developer education from companies like Frontend Masters, Epic Web, Build UI, ByteByteGo, The Odin Project, Full Stack Open, and other respected engineering learning resources. * Learn how experienced educators introduce concepts, reduce cognitive load, and maintain engagement. * Apply those ideas while keeping the course original. Do not copy content. Use research only to improve structure, sequencing, pedagogy, and learning experience. --- # Existing Course The current course is a Multi-Vendor Food Marketplace project. Do not change the project itself. Improve how it is taught. The project should become the vehicle through which students learn software engineering. --- # Expected Quality Imagine this course is being compared against the best paid software engineering courses in the world. Every lesson should feel polished. Every explanation should have purpose. Every transition should feel intentional. Every module should prepare students for the next one. The final result should feel cohesive, professional, modern, and production-oriented—not like a collection of tutorials, but like a complete engineering curriculum that takes a student from zero to confidently building and shipping a real-world product. please improve it"}
